323:referent: `example-fixes-i1-green.out`: `chain_attacks.lua` A1-A8 all HOLDS (A1 "new-id bastila min_one_hp=0"), `pc_probe.lua` P1-P3 all HOLDS (P3 "new-id PC min_one_hp=0"). A1 passes although it keeps the stale party roster `{1,2}`, because of the `GetObjectByTag` fallback (party members only).
328:referent: `example-fixes-i1-mutations.out`. Mutant 1 (no carry-over of a pending prior in `guard`) fails only "stale guard entry made the stuck flag the new prior". Mutant 2 (no `GetFirstPC` branch) fails the PC test and the missing-member test. Mutant 3 (no restore call) fails 4 cases.
332:What the script now does (I1). `restore_guards` no longer trusts pre-transition IDs. `current(id, entry)` resolves the PC through `GetFirstPC`. It resolves a party member by the tag stored at guard time: first through `GetPartyMemberCount`/`GetPartyMemberByIndex`, then `GetObjectByTag` if `IsObjectPartyMember`. A stored ID is used only if it is still valid and its tag still matches. An entry that cannot be resolved is printed as `[cursed:boom] min-one-HP restore pending: <tag|PC> not found` and kept for the next settle, area enter or module load. Every other entry is restored in the same pass. If a blast reaches a pending creature under its new ID, `guard` carries the stored prior over instead of reading the stuck 1. All engine access stays in `q`/`call` over `nwscript.call`; `boundary_scan.py` reports 0 collisions for the new bytes. Header and README:13 say what is guaranteed, and that a save written inside the 3 s window and loaded in a new session is not covered.
352:Note for later (no code change): `OnAreaEnter` fires for any entering object (review A8). While a guarded member cannot be found, every creature entry and every settle prints "restore pending" again, which can repeat in the log if that member left the game for good.
